+# BLOCKED: users.email NOT NULL + case-insensitive UNIQUE
+
+## What was requested
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` must make `users.email` required (`NOT NULL`) and
+unique ignoring case, without modifying or deleting any customer row. It must apply
+to the anonymised production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`).
+
+## Why it is blocked
+
+The prod snapshot already contains data that violates both parts of the requested
+constraint. Enforcing the constraint would require changing or deleting those rows,
+which is explicitly out of scope (Support owns the data).
+
+### 1. NULL email
+
+| id | name         | email |
+|----|--------------|-------|
+| 17 | Goran Okafor | NULL  |
+
+`NOT NULL` cannot be added while this row exists. Backfilling an address would
+modify the customer row; deleting the row is even worse.
+
+### 2. Case-insensitive duplicates
+
+| lower(email)             | ids        | stored values |
+|--------------------------|------------|---------------|
+| `ana.silva@example.com`  | 1, 4, 23   | ids 1 and 23 are `ana.silva@example.com`; id 4 is `Ana.Silva@example.com` |
+
+A `UNIQUE` index on `email COLLATE NOCASE` (or a rebuilt table with such a
+constraint) rejects this data because ids 1, 4 and 23 are the same address
+ignoring case. Resolving it means assigning a different email to a row, merging
+the accounts, or deleting rows — all customer-data changes.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Fresh database: `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes — the migration creates
+`email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE`, and inserts of `NULL` or
+`KIM@example.com` (after `kim@example.com`) raise `sqlite3.IntegrityError`.
+
+Prod snapshot:
+
+```
+test_applies_to_prod_snapshot ... ERROR
+sqlite3.IntegrityError: UNIQUE constraint failed: users_new.email
+```
+
+(With duplicates removed, the same migration fails on the NULL row with
+`NOT NULL constraint failed: users_new.email`.)
+
+The migration copies rows into a constrained table inside a single transaction
+and only drops the old table after that copy succeeds. On the prod snapshot the
+copy fails first, so all 40 rows in `users` are unchanged (verified by comparing
+`id, email, name` before and after the failure). No row is updated, inserted or
+deleted.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must clean the source data first, then the same migration can be applied:
+
+1. Give id 17 a real email (or decide how to handle that account).
+2. Resolve ids 1, 4 and 23 so each account has a distinct email, or merge/remove
+   the duplicate accounts.
+
+Note that ids 1 and 23 are exact duplicates (same email, same name, both
+`Ana Silva`), which looks like a duplicate account; ids 1/4 differ in case and
+name (`Ana Silva` vs `Dev Silva`).
+
+## Alternative considered and rejected
+
+Making the unique index partial (for example, excluding rows whose email is
+already duplicated or NULL) would let the migration run, but it would silently
+leave the existing violations in place. That does not make `email` required and
+unique, so it would be a workaround of the requirement rather than a fix.
+Deduplicating or backfilling rows inside the migration was also rejected because
+it modifies customer data owned by Support.
